<section class="editorial-hero bleed-full editorial-hero--image"><div class="container editorial-hero__inner"><div class="editorial-hero__copy"><p class="kicker">Kids karate / Ages 7+</p><h1>A place to begin.</h1><div class="editorial-hero__intro"><p>Karate for children aged 7 and above. Respect, concentration and coordination are part of learning traditional Wado-Ryu, one technique at a time.</p><p>Complete beginners are welcome. Your child can try a suitable class without booking, and you can stay to watch every lesson.</p></div><a class="cta-button cta-button--primary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/classes/kids/')); ?>" data-discover="true">Find a children's class</a></div><figure class="editorial-hero__image"><?php hakuba_hero_image('images/optimized/facebook-grading-practice-1280.webp', 'Young Hakuba students practising a controlled partner technique', 1280, 960); ?></figure></div></section><dl class="first-visit-facts" aria-label="Your child's first visit"><div><dt>From age 7</dt><dd>No karate experience needed.</dd></div><div><dt>Parents can stay</dt><dd>Watch every lesson, not just the first.</dd></div><div><dt>No kit to buy first</dt><dd>Comfortable clothes and water are enough.</dd></div></dl><section class="editorial-split editorial-split--reverse"><figure class="editorial-split__image"><img src="<?php echo esc_url(hakuba_asset('images/optimized/pad-practice-march-2026-1280.webp')); ?>" srcset="<?php echo esc_attr(hakuba_srcset('/images/optimized/pad-practice-march-2026-640.webp 640w, /images/optimized/pad-practice-march-2026-1280.webp 1280w')); ?>" sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 560px, (min-width: 700px) 46vw, 92vw" alt="A young Hakuba student practising a kick into a focus mitt held by a training partner" width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Learning control through practice.</figcaption></figure><div class="editorial-split__copy"><p class="kicker">Learning through practice</p><h2>The basics come first.</h2><p>Children learn rei (respect), kihon (basic techniques) and structured movement. Regular practice gives them time to work on each skill.</p><p>Instructors offer supportive coaching and guidance on progression. You can ask your instructor about the class pathway when you arrive.</p><a class="cta-button cta-button--secondary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/beginners')); ?>" data-discover="true">What happens in a first class?</a></div></section><section class="training-focus" aria-labelledby="children-training-title"><div class="training-focus__heading"><div><p class="kicker">Learning beyond the belt</p><h2 id="children-training-title">Small skills. Steady practice.</h2></div><p>Every child starts somewhere. Traditional karate gives them a routine in which to practise concentration, movement and consideration for other people.</p></div><dl class="training-focus__list"><div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Focus and listening</dt><dd>Following an instruction, watching a demonstration and trying again are part of each lesson. Children have something specific to concentrate on, one movement at a time.</dd></div><div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Coordination and control</dt><dd>Stances, footwork and basic techniques give children a chance to practise balance and controlled movement. Getting the basics right matters more than rushing ahead.</dd></div><div><dt><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Respect for others</dt><dd>Bowing, listening and consideration for training partners are part of learning karate. Progress is about how children practise as well as the techniques they learn.</dd></div></dl></section><section id="confidence-and-bullying" class="editorial-band bleed-full"><div class="container editorial-band__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Confidence / Respect / Self-control</p><h2>Confidence, with<br>consideration.</h2><p>Karate is not about looking for a fight. Children practise listening, working with others and controlling their movements.</p></div><div><h3>If bullying is part of the picture</h3><p>Tell us why you are considering karate for your child. We can discuss the class and what training involves, without promising a quick fix.</p><p>Karate does not replace support from a school or a trusted adult. A child does not have to deal with bullying alone, and learning techniques is not a reason to seek confrontation.</p><a class="cta-button cta-button--primary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/contact?interest=Kids')); ?>" data-discover="true">Ask about a class for your child</a></div></div></section><section class="pathway-split"><article><p class="kicker">Wroughton / Swindon</p><h2>Junior and mixed sessions.</h2><p>Our junior and mixed sessions welcome children aged 7+. View the class times to choose a suitable session, then arrive a few minutes early. No booking is needed.</p><a class="cta-button cta-button--secondary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/classes/kids')); ?>" data-discover="true">Junior session details</a></article><article><p class="kicker">Mevagissey / Cornwall</p><h2>A family class from age 7.</h2><p>Children from age 7 can join the family sessions in Mevagissey. Check the class times and arrive a few minutes early. You can contact us if you would like help choosing.</p><a class="cta-button cta-button--secondary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/locations/mevagissey')); ?>" data-discover="true">Explore Mevagissey dojo</a></article></section><?php hakuba_class_link(); ?><section><h2>Questions from parents</h2><details class="faq-item"><summary>Can parents stay and watch?</summary><p>Yes. Parents are welcome to stay and watch every lesson, not just the first.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What age can my child start?</summary><p>Children can join from age 7. Find junior, mixed and family sessions in the current timetable. Ask the club which is most suitable.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Do I need to buy a gi before the first class?</summary><p>No. Your child can try karate in comfortable training clothing. Bring water and arrive a few minutes early to meet the instructor. No booking is needed.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What if my child is nervous?</summary><p>Arrive a few minutes early so your child can meet the instructor before the session starts. You can stay to watch, and you are welcome to contact us beforehand if there is anything you would like to discuss.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Can karate help if my child is being bullied?</summary><p>Training gives children an opportunity to work on confidence, respect and controlled movement. It is not a guarantee against bullying or a substitute for support from their school or a trusted adult. Tell us what you hope your child will get from training.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How does belt progression work?</summary><p>The grading syllabus sets out the techniques for each grade. Your child learns through regular practice, with their instructor guiding progression. There is no need to know the syllabus before the first class.</p></details></section><section class="join-band bleed-full"><div class="container join-band__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Wroughton / Mevagissey</p><h2>A first step, at their pace.</h2><p>Choose a suitable class and come a few minutes early. No booking or karate uniform needed. Questions first? We are happy to help.</p></div><a class="cta-button cta-button--primary" href="<?php echo esc_url(home_url('/classes/#class-times')); ?>" data-discover="true">View children's class times <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a></div></section>
